      "id": 1,
      "text": "Williams Canyon takes its name from Marshall Williams, a bachelor beekeeper who settled there before 1888.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ochistoryland.com/williamscyn",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Silverado Canyon lies to the north and Modjeska Canyon to the south, framing Williams Canyon between mining and theatrical history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ochistoryland.com/williamscyn",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1888, Polish‑born actress Helena Modjeska bought a cabin in Williams Canyon and expanded it into a summer retreat.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ochistoryland.com/williamscyn",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Higuera family, first recorded in the 1870s, were famed for riding, roping, and a legendary love of cheap wine.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ochistoryland.com/williamscyn",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Most of Williams Canyon sits inside Cleveland National Forest, protecting its rugged hills and wildlife.",
